 "summary": {
  "text": "The sample shop dataset records the activity of a retail platform from mid-2023 through the end of 2025. It contains a customers table (one row per 5,012 customers) linked to regions and, when present, to a marketing_campaigns table; orders (50,021 rows) reference customers and regions; each order is broken into order_items (80,355 rows) that point to products (120 rows). Payments (48,806 rows) attach to orders, and product reviews (23,088 rows) join to both customers and products. Marketing campaigns (11 rows) are defined by start and end dates and budget. The time dimension spans signup dates from July 2023 to November 2025, and transactional dates from January 2024 to December 2025. Analysts should note that acquisition_campaign_id is missing for more than half of customers and cost_usd is absent in 12.5 % of products, so joins may produce nulls. Several numeric fields show notable outlier rates—unit_price (≈9.5 %), quantity (15 %), discount_pct (14 %) and total_amount (≈6 %)—which can distort averages. Finally, the data are heavily imbalanced: the Standard customer segment accounts for over half of the base, 96 % of orders are delivered, and returns occur in only about 9 % of line items, so any segmentation or return-rate analysis must account for these skewed distributions.",
  "model": "openai.gpt-oss-120b",
  "ai_generated": true
 },
